d per iteration? No: The variable is declared once per loop body execution; but since the loop body is executed multiple times sequentially within a thread, fx,fy,fz will be reused across iterations for that thread. That's fine because they are reset each time. So it's effectively private to each thread (since each thread has its own stack). But OpenMP does not require explicit privatization in this case because the variable is defined inside the loop body and thus automatically private per iteration? Actually, according to OpenMP specification, variables declared within a parallel region or worksharing construct are private by default. So it's fine.\n\nWhen we move to single parallel region with separate for constructs, we need to ensure that fx,fy,fz are still private. We can declare them inside the force loop block as before.\n\nNow, let's write the new code. We'll keep body_force function but inline its content into main? Or we can keep it and call from within parallel region? If we have a single parallel region outside iteration loop, calling body_force would cause each thread to execute the entire function? That would be wrong because body_force contains #pragma omp for which is not allowed inside another worksharing construct. So we must inline the force computation into main.\n\nThus: Remove body_force function and put its code directly in main loop, with appropriate OpenMP directives.\n\nAlso need to handle position update similarly.\n\nWe'll also consider using schedule(guided) as before but maybe adjust chunk size? Guided scheduling doesn't take a chunk size; it's automatic. But we can use schedule(dynamic,64) which was best in one experiment. Let's try dynamic with chunk 64 for force loop and static for position update (since trivial). Or guided for both.\n\nGiven the goal includes \"reductions\", maybe we could try to use reduction on forces? Not applicable.\n\nAnother idea: Use #pragma omp parallel for schedule(guided) collapse(2)? No, loops are not pe